#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068623 :question/id #uuid "4b9a93ba-ed6c-525f-8930-cb68efd6709e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6085" :question/problem "Find the slope of each of the lines below.\n\n![](images/e9/e901704deb02efe957129512148c97e42b45b4fdd1d0e3af451562cca2ed1181.png)" :question/worked-solution "The slope of the vertical line is undefined. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(4,0)$ and $(4,1),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{1-0}{4-4}=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the horizontal line is zero. We can see this by choosing two points on the line, such as $(0,3)$ and $(1,3),$ and computing the slope between them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{3-3}{1-0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068669 :question/id #uuid "0b380dbf-80da-582c-a2cd-ab675791c8b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-988" :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48114216e210a4e4dfc353871bbfd16bd2beb480f5831c62189db9299e09f517.png)" :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2).$ So, we let $(x_1,y_1) = (-2,0)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (0,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-0}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068670 :question/id #uuid "d086fc71-f668-5ce7-8fd0-b046a3c228eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-989" :question/problem "Calculate the slope of the line that passes through the points $(1, 5)$ and $(4, 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We let $(x_1,y_1) = (1,5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,2),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\tm &= \\dfrac {y_2 - y_1} {x_2 - x_1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {2 - 5} {4 - 1} \\\\\n\t\t    &= \\dfrac {-3}{3} \\\\\n\t\t    &= -1\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the slope is $m=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068671 :question/id #uuid "7a806653-8d1d-543c-91de-94b9e892b88b" :question/math-academy-id "e-990" :question/problem "Given the following graph, what can we say about the sign of the slope?\n\n![](images/ff/ffa6dd91f72bc66c943e88aa764a64811391a00af162b40320ce3d90f96b16a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that slope is the amount the line rises vertically per unit that it runs horizontally.\n\nSince the graph decreases ($\\searrow$) as we go from left to right, the line rises by a negative amount.\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a76e0d6d-c9ae-5959-9c10-52d52cb5fd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065932} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e1ccad-ba65-5470-9eaa-a82acde3bbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065933} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ba7cfb-c160-560f-8c2b-4a9995c134c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065934} {:answer/id #uuid "cc65e549-bc14-5dac-abf9-0ee1d5881a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065935} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9f2105-55a5-5f56-9957-6a7266c07632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065936}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e5326276-b054-51f0-8db7-de2e8a381a19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065931}] :question/id #uuid "729fb737-c438-5599-8917-516731af867a" :question/math-a